---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';


const SYSLOG = `# On the device, point syslog at the 24Observe ingest endpoint
# (FortiGate example — concept is the same for Palo Alto, Cisco, etc.)
config log syslogd setting
  set status enable
  set server "<your-ingest-endpoint>"
  set port 514
end`;

const POLL = `Device:     core-switch-2
Address:    10.0.0.2
Profile:    switch (interfaces, throughput, errors, saturation)
Interval:   60s
Credentials: stored encrypted, scoped to read-only polling`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What does onboarding a network device give me?',
    a: 'Two streams of signal. Syslog from firewalls and network gear becomes searchable, normalised security events that the detection packs run over — so a firewall block or a config change is part of your SIEM. And SNMP polling turns the device into live metric series — interface status, throughput, errors, saturation — that you can chart and threshold-alert on. Together they make a device a first-class citizen of both your monitoring and your security.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I send a device’s logs in?',
    a: 'Point the device\'s syslog at the ingest endpoint. Firewalls and network operating systems — FortiGate, Palo Alto, Cisco, and others — all support forwarding syslog, and once configured, their events flow in and are normalised to a shared security schema so detections behave consistently regardless of vendor. It is a configuration change on the device, not an agent install.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does SNMP metric polling work?',
    a: 'The collector polls the device over SNMP on an interval and turns the readings into metric series in the platform. You provide the device address and read-only SNMP credentials, choose a profile appropriate to the device type, and the relevant counters — interfaces, throughput, errors — start arriving as series you can alert on. The credentials are stored encrypted and scoped to read-only polling.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which vendors are supported?',
    a: 'The syslog path works with any device that can forward syslog, which is effectively all enterprise network gear, and common vendors — FortiGate, Palo Alto, Cisco — are normalised to the shared schema so their events are consistent. SNMP polling works with standard device profiles. The approach is vendor-neutral by design: open protocols in, normalised data out.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do network events get detections and investigation?',
    a: 'Yes. Normalised firewall and device events feed the same detection engine as everything else, including the infrastructure-and-network health pack, and any match opens an incident the analyst investigates. So a suspicious firewall pattern or a device health problem is not just a log line — it is a detection that becomes an investigated incident, alongside your application and host security.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I alert when a device or interface is in trouble?',
    a: 'Yes. Once a device is polled, its metrics are series like any other, so you set threshold alerts on them — a saturating interface, a rising error counter, a link going down — exactly as you would on a host\'s disk or a service\'s latency. See the metric &amp; SNMP alerting guide for the threshold setup.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does this show up on the topology map?',
    a: 'Polled devices become nodes on the context graph with a live health state, so a switch or router is part of your blast-radius picture. When a device fails and takes downstream hosts with it, the platform can see that the resulting incidents share that device as a root and group them into one case — so a network failure that cascades pages you about the device, once, not about every symptom.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is this only for big network teams?',
    a: 'No. Any team running their own network gear — a few switches and a firewall in a rack, or a full estate — benefits from having device logs and metrics in the same place as their applications and hosts. It is especially valuable for NOC and ops teams, but the setup is light enough that a small team can onboard a device in a few minutes. See the NOC solution for the broader picture.',
  },
];
